[Music]  where did the names of the months come  from  they come like so many things from the  romans  before the norman conquest of england in  1066 the anglo-saxons had their own  names for the months but the latin names  became more and more used throughout  europe during the high middle ages  eventually they supplanted the native  names in places like france germany and  england okay so we have to go back to  rome the original roman calendar had  only 10 months consisting of 304 days  the two missing months were in winter  which was a monthless part of the year  and so the original calendar started in  march and that's where we'll start this  will be helpful as we go along trust me  before we continue here's a quick word  from our sponsor and that sponsor is  babel have you ever wanted to learn a  new language or maybe brush up on one  that you learned in high school or  college but you just don't know where to  start  well then let me introduce you to babel  babel is the premier language learning  app on the market it teaches real world  practical conversations and easy to  digest 10 minute lessons i'm using it to  brush up on the french i learned in  college bonjour jesui le pepe geneliste  pebel  how was my accent need some work well  that's no problem  because babel uses speech recognition  software to help improve your  pronunciation and accent i know my  viewers are always looking to learn new  things so whether you're traveling to  new lands trying to connect with  non-english speakers in your community  or just want to improve yourself why not  give babble a try click the link in the  description to get 65 off your first  purchase thank you again to babel and  now back to the video  so march or martius in the original  latin was named for mars the roman god  of war  mars was equivalent to ares in greece  if you know about greek paganism he  wasn't very respected in greece seen as  a god of bloodshed and violence in rome  though he was much more revered seen as  one of the most important of the twelve  olympians the romans associated mars  with strategy and intelligence rather  than bloodshed and was believed to have  been the father of romulus and remus the  legendary founders of the city of rome  april or aprilis is our first month with  uncertain etymology  it could be named for aphrodite the  greek goddess of love and beauty  the romans called her venus which seems  to cast some doubt on this explanation  but she was called a prue by the  etruscans so maybe that explains it  the other explanation is that april is  connected with the latin verb aperi re  which means to open this could refer to  the opening or beginning of spring  or to the opening or blooming of flowers  quick side note the anglo-saxon name for  this month was eastermonith named for  the goddess wester  she was a fertility goddess in the  germanic pantheon of gods this is where  we get our word for the christian  holiday easter  the month of may also has an uncertain  etymology but it's mostly agreed that it  was named for the goddess maya maya was  a minor goddess in roman paganism but  she was the mother of a very important  god mercury mercury was the herald of  the gods and the deity associated with  travelers merchants messengers and  thieves he was also associated with may  so that lends credence to this theory  there is another explanation though this  one given to us by ovid one of the most  celebrated roman poets  he thought that the month was named for  the mayors which in latin meant elders  so may was for the elder citizens of  rome while june was for the youth or in  latin juniores  speaking of let's move on to june as i  said ovid thought the month was named  for the young people of rome but most  scholars disagree instead it's believed  that june was named for juno the goddess  of motherhood and child rearing she was  the queen of the olympians and wife to  jupiter  this to me seems a more likely  explanation than ovid's theory now  moving on to july and august these two  months were renamed at the start of the  imperial period of roman history to  honor and deify two roman emperors  julius caesar and caesar augustus and  yes i know julius caesar wasn't  technically emperor he held the title  dictator but same difference so july  named for julius used to be named  quintillus which meant fifth month  remember we're counting for march as  month one and august was named for  augustus the first true emperor of rome  it had the name sextilus meaning sixth  month thank goodness it was renamed or  we would have another uranus on our  hands there's a common myth about august  that you may have heard the story goes  like this augustus wanted to be seen as  equal or greater to his uncle julius  caesar so he made it so that his month  had 31 days the same as july he did this  by taking a day from february and giving  it to august and thus messing up the  nice alternating system of 30 and 31  dayed months  this myth is not true  or at least there's no evidence to  support it august did at one point have  only 30 days but the number of days in  each month changed a number of times  throughout roman history and it seems  like sextillis already had 31 days by  the time it was renamed so no you can't  blame augustus for making you forget how  many days are in each month the next  four months are unremarkable they're  named for numbers september meaning  seventh month october meaning 8th  november 9th and december the 10th and  last month of the original system  last two months were added in the 8th  century bc by numa pompilius the second  king of rome  january was named for the many headed  god giannis he was the god of  transitions and doorways which is  fitting for the new first month of the  year  interestingly he was a specifically  italian god having no greek equivalent  and finally we have february it is the  only month not named for a god or number  at least if you discount ovid's theories  about may and june  february was named for a festival that  occurred in its days called februa it  was a roman purification festival held  in and around the lupercull cave where  it was believed romulus and remus were  nursed to health by a she-wolf the  festival was also called lupercalia for  this reason  so there you are the origins of the  names of the months  hope you enjoyed  again i'd like to thank babel for  sponsoring this video and thank you to  my patrons if you want to support the  channel directly please consider  supporting said patreon the link is in  the description if not please like and  subscribe  bye  [Music]

